    <div class="jr-answer">
      <h2>The short answer</h2>
      <p><strong>Knee cracking and popping is usually harmless.</strong> Pooled across more than 100 studies, 36% of people with no knee pain at all have audible crepitus, so the noise on its own is not a diagnosis. It matters when it arrives with pain, swelling, locking or giving way — those need an examination.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>How common:</strong> around 41% of knees in the general population, and 36% of entirely pain-free knees, make noise (British Journal of Sports Medicine, 2025).</li>
        <li><strong>The arthritis link is real but weak:</strong> crepitus roughly triples the odds of radiographic osteoarthritis on a scan, yet most people who have it never develop painful arthritis.</li>
        <li><strong>What changes the answer:</strong> pain, swelling within hours, a knee that locks or buckles, or a noise that started at the moment of an injury.</li>
        <li><strong>The honest limitation:</strong> nothing reliably stops the sound — not exercise, and not any supplement, including ours.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

