    <div class="jr-prose">

      <h2 id="why">Why do my knees crack and pop?</h2>

      <p>
        Most knee noise comes from gas and geometry, not from damage. Nobody can tell you with
        certainty which mechanism is making your particular sound, because the research has never
        matched a specific noise to a specific structure in a living knee.
      </p>

      <p>Four mechanisms account for nearly all of it:</p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Cavitation.</strong> When joint surfaces are pulled apart, pressure inside drops
          and a gas cavity forms in the synovial fluid. Real-time MRI work suggests the crack is the
          cavity <em>forming</em> rather than a bubble collapsing, which is why the same joint
          usually will not crack again for 15 to 20 minutes — the gas needs time to redissolve.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Soft tissue snapping over bone.</strong> Tendons and the iliotibial band slide
          over bony ridges as the knee bends. If one catches and releases, you get a clean snap at
          the same angle every time.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Kneecap tracking.</strong> The patella runs in a groove on the femur, and slight
          differences in how it enters that groove produce the gritty crepitus most people notice
          when they squat or come down stairs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rougher joint surfaces.</strong> In a knee with established osteoarthritis,
          cartilage is thinner and less smooth, and that change can be felt and heard. This is the
          mechanism people are frightened of, and the least likely one if you have no pain.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="common">How common is a noisy knee?</h2>

      <p>
        Common enough that it is closer to normal than to abnormal. The best available summary is a
        2025 systematic review and meta-analysis in the <em>British Journal of Sports Medicine</em>
        by Couch and colleagues, which pooled 103 studies from 28 countries.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-stats">
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">41%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of knees in the general population have crepitus (7,609 knees pooled)</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=noisy+knees+crepitus+prevalence+structural+pathology+meta-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Br J Sports Med, 2025</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">36%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of entirely pain-free knees make noise (95% CI 23–50%, 852 knees)</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=noisy+knees+crepitus+prevalence+structural+pathology+meta-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Br J Sports Med, 2025</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="jr-stat">
          <p class="jr-stat-num">61%</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-lbl">of knees with known cartilage pathology (95% CI 40–81%, 1,445 knees)</p>
          <p class="jr-stat-src"><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term=noisy+knees+crepitus+prevalence+structural+pathology+meta-analysis" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">Br J Sports Med, 2025</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        Read those three numbers together and the useful point emerges. Yes, noise is more common in
        damaged knees — 61% against 36%. But a third of healthy, painless knees make it too, and 39%
        of knees with real cartilage damage are silent. A noise is a weak signal in both directions.
        The review's own authors flag that the certainty of this evidence is low to very low, with
        enormous variation between studies, so these are rough orders of magnitude rather than
        precise figures.
      </p>

      <h2 id="arthritis">Does knee cracking mean arthritis?</h2>

      <p>
        It raises the odds measurably, and it is still a poor individual predictor. Both halves of
        that sentence are true, and most pages online give you only one of them.
      </p>

      <p>
        The 2025 review found crepitus carried more than three times the odds of a radiographic
        osteoarthritis diagnosis, along with several osteoarthritis-related MRI features. A
        longitudinal study exists too: Lo and colleagues, in <em>Arthritis Care &amp; Research</em>
        in 2018, followed Osteoarthritis Initiative participants with at least one knee free of
        symptomatic arthritis. The more often people reported crepitus, the more likely symptomatic
        knee osteoarthritis became over the next four years — adjusted odds ratios of 1.5 for
        "rarely", 1.8 for "sometimes", 2.2 for "often" and 3.0 for "always".
      </p>

      <p>
        Here is the part that gets left out. In that same study, most new symptomatic cases occurred
        in people who already had radiographic osteoarthritis but no frequent pain yet. Crepitus was
        largely flagging arthritis already visible on a scan, not predicting disease in a
        structurally normal knee. And an odds ratio of 3.0 on an uncommon outcome still leaves most
        noisy-kneed people without symptomatic arthritis.
      </p>

      <p>
        A 2026 study in the same journal, from the group behind the 2025 review, found crepitus was
        <em>not</em> associated with worsening osteoarthritis features for up to five years after
        anterior cruciate ligament reconstruction in young adults. Different population, so no
        contradiction — but the noise cannot be read the same way in a 25-year-old post-surgical
        knee as in a 65-year-old one. For how joints deteriorate and why repair is slow, see
        <a href="/pages/collagen-for-joint-pain">collagen for joint pain</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="which">Which noises are worth worrying about?</h2>

      <p>
        The pattern around the noise carries far more information than the noise itself. This is the
        table I draw for patients in clinic.
      </p>

      <div class="jr-table-scroll">
        <table class="jr-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Reassuring pattern</th><th scope="col">Needs an examination</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Pain</th><td>None, at the time or after</td><td>Pain with the noise, or pain that follows it</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Swelling</th><td>None</td><td>Any swelling, especially within hours</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Movement</th><td>Full range, knee straightens completely</td><td>Catches, locks, or will not fully straighten</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Stability</th><td>Steady on uneven ground and stairs</td><td>Gives way, buckles, or feels unreliable</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Onset</th><td>Gradual, present for years, unchanged</td><td>Started at the moment of a twist, fall or tackle</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Trend</th><td>Stable over months</td><td>Clearly worse over weeks, with new symptoms</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        A single loud pop at the instant of an injury, followed by swelling over the next few hours,
        is the one presentation that should not wait. That combination is how a cruciate ligament
        tear, a significant meniscal tear or a fracture announces itself, because rapid swelling
        usually means bleeding inside the joint.
      </p>

      <h2 id="doctor">When should a noisy knee be checked?</h2>

      <p>Book an appointment, rather than reading more pages, if you have:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>A pop or snap that happened during an injury, particularly a twisting one.</li>
        <li>Swelling that came on within a few hours of an injury.</li>
        <li>A knee that locks, catches, or cannot be straightened fully.</li>
        <li>A knee that gives way under you, or that you do not trust on stairs.</li>
        <li>Pain that is limiting what you do, whatever the noise is doing.</li>
        <li>Redness, heat or fever with a swollen knee — that needs same-day assessment, not an appointment next week.</li>
        <li>Noise and symptoms that are clearly getting worse over a few weeks.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        A painless, stable, full-range knee that has crackled for years generally needs neither an
        X-ray nor an MRI. Scanning it is more likely to find an incidental, meaningless finding than
        to change anything you do.
      </p>

      <h2 id="helps">What actually helps a noisy knee?</h2>

      <p>
        Nothing reliably switches the sound off, and you should be sceptical of anyone who says
        otherwise. What the evidence supports is making the knee stronger and more comfortable while
        the noise carries on in the background.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Stop treating the noise as damage.</strong> Qualitative research on people with
          patellofemoral pain found crepitus provokes real fear and drives avoidance — people stop
          squatting, stop stairs, stop training. That avoidance deconditions the knee and tends to
          make symptoms worse. Reassurance is not a soft option here; it is the intervention with
          the clearest mechanism.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Build quadriceps and hip strength.</strong> Exercise therapy is the mainstay of
          management for anterior knee pain and knee osteoarthritis — by improving pain and
          function, not by silencing the joint. Reviews suggest crepitus in established
          osteoarthritis is unlikely to change, which is why you can be told to carry on regardless.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Work in ranges that do not hurt.</strong> If deep squatting grinds and aches,
          partial-range and isometric work builds the same muscle without the provocation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reduce load where you can.</strong> Body weight is the biggest modifiable
          mechanical factor in knee osteoarthritis — and again, it affects pain, not noise.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Do not try to make it crack.</strong> Deliberately cracking a joint for relief has
          no evidence behind it, and forcing the sound out risks the soft tissue you are protecting.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        The belief that cracking causes arthritis entered common sense through knuckles, and even
        there it was never supported: a 2011 study in the <em>Journal of the American Board of
        Family Medicine</em> compared 215 adults and found hand osteoarthritis in 18.1% of habitual
        knuckle crackers against 21.5% of non-crackers. Different joint, and a voluntary habit
        rather than an involuntary noise, so it does not transfer directly to knees.
      </p>

      <h2 id="india">Does squatting and floor sitting make it worse?</h2>

      <p>
        Prolonged squatting probably carries some risk; sitting on the floor appears not to. This
        matters in India because both are daily life rather than lifestyle choices, and because knee
        osteoarthritis is common here — a 2025 meta-analysis of Indian population-based studies
        pooled prevalence at 20.2% of adults (95% CI 11.9–30.1), and a separate 2025 meta-analysis
        of 14 studies in 5,029 people aged 60 and over put it at 47% (95% CI 38.4–55.8).
      </p>

      <p>
        The clearest evidence on floor-based living comes from a Tehran COPCORD study in
        <em>Arthritis &amp; Rheumatism</em> in 2009, which matched 480 people with knee
        osteoarthritis to controls from the same community. Adjusted for age, sex and BMI, prolonged
        squatting was associated with knee osteoarthritis (odds ratio 1.51, 95% CI 1.12–2.04) and so
        was cycling (2.06, 95% CI 1.23–3.45), while prolonged standing, walking up and down hills,
        and — notably — <em>sitting on the floor</em> were not.
      </p>

      <p>
        Two caveats before anyone rearranges their house. That was an Iranian cohort, not an Indian
        one, though floor-based living is broadly comparable. And it is case-control, so reverse
        causation is live: people whose knees already hurt stop squatting. The practical reading is
        modest. If a deep squat or an Indian-style toilet reliably hurts, use an alternative. If it
        only makes a noise, that is not an argument for changing anything.
      </p>

      <h2 id="supplements">Does any supplement stop knees cracking?</h2>

      <p>
        No, and no trial has even tried. Joint noise has never been a primary outcome in a
        randomised trial of collagen, glucosamine, chondroitin or any other supplement, so there is
        no dose, no timeline and no effect size to quote. Any page that tells you a capsule will
        quieten your knees is making it up.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is worth stating plainly from a company that sells a joint supplement. Our
        <a href="/pages/supplements-for-knee-pain">guide to supplements for knee pain</a> maps what
        the evidence does and does not support for <em>pain</em>, and even there the conclusion is
        that exercise outperforms everything on the page. Noise sits a level below: not merely
        unproven, but unstudied.
      </p>

      <p>
        Skelecoll is formulated for joint and bone support at trial-level collagen dosing, not as a
