      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="jr-faq">
        <details>
          <summary>Is it bad if my knees crack when I squat?</summary>
          <p>Not by itself. Around 36% of entirely pain-free knees produce audible crepitus, so noise during a squat is a common finding rather than a sign of damage. If the squat also hurts, swells the knee, or makes it catch, that is worth an examination.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does knee cracking mean I am losing cartilage?</summary>
          <p>Usually not. Crepitus is more common in knees with cartilage pathology, at around 61% compared with 36% of pain-free knees, but 39% of knees with real cartilage damage are completely silent. The sound is a weak signal in both directions and cannot tell you the state of your cartilage.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Will my noisy knees turn into arthritis?</summary>
          <p>Most will not. A 2018 study in Arthritis Care and Research found the risk of developing symptomatic knee osteoarthritis rose with how often people reported crepitus, from an adjusted odds ratio of 1.5 to 3.0. Most new cases, though, occurred in knees that already showed osteoarthritis on X-ray without frequent pain.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Should I stop exercising if my knee makes noise?</summary>
          <p>No. Avoiding movement because of the sound tends to decondition the knee and make symptoms worse. Exercise therapy is the mainstay of management for anterior knee pain and knee osteoarthritis. Work in ranges that do not hurt, and let the noise carry on.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can collagen or glucosamine stop my knees cracking?</summary>
          <p>There is no evidence that any supplement reduces joint noise, because no trial has used crepitus as an outcome. Skelecoll contains 10,000mg of hydrolysed marine collagen and 300mg of glucosamine HCl for joint and bone support, and neither is a treatment for the sound.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does cracking your joints cause arthritis?</summary>
          <p>The evidence, which comes from hands rather than knees, says no. A 2011 study of 215 adults found hand osteoarthritis in 18.1% of habitual knuckle crackers against 21.5% of non-crackers. Deliberately forcing a knee to crack is still a bad idea, because of the soft tissue you can strain doing it.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>When should I see a doctor about a noisy knee?</summary>
          <p>See a doctor if the noise started during an injury, if the knee swelled within a few hours, if it locks, catches or will not straighten, if it gives way, or if pain is limiting what you do. A swollen knee with redness, heat or fever needs same-day assessment.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Is squatting or sitting cross-legged bad for my knees?</summary>
          <p>A Tehran COPCORD study found prolonged squatting was associated with knee osteoarthritis at an odds ratio of 1.51, while sitting on the floor was not a risk factor. If a position reliably hurts, use an alternative. If it only makes a noise, that is not a reason to change anything.</p>
        </details>
      </div>

